    "c1": {
      "anchor": "spaces#markdown-3",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "3. Spaces and roles",
      "excerpt": "In our own data, who holds a role is always looked up, never copied. A project n",
      "text": "Convert to a markdown table with the bold bit in the left column wrapped in <nobr>.  Also for stuff in the next section",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-02T01:00:46.894Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "What's in it: who holds each role, the rules around roles, which apps may be installed and at which versions, and a record of changes to any of these.\nAdmins are the super-admins of the setup, as in real life. They set things up, and the Board runs the org.\nEveryone else changes it through Spell, under its rules: a project's Lead adds a Lead, a Membership Team Lead changes someone's role, and the write server checks the rules and writes the change.\nWhat others need to see is projected: the roster goes to the Membership Team's public space, and each space's Leads to that space.\nSpell reads it to check writes, which isn't gating a read: it's looking up its own rules. Who can read an ATSpace is still AT Protocol's.\nApps: Leads install only what's on the Admins' approved list, at the version listed, so one app can't change under an org by surprise.\nNo secrets in it: whoever runs the host can read it, as nothing is encrypted, so sign-in grants and mail passwords stay out.\nWho watches the Admins: Admins name other Admins, and the Board holds the org's recovery key, so it can step in if the last Admin leaves or things go wrong.\nAdmin requests: Admins also have a public space, e.g. listing available apps to install, where anyone in the org can ask them for something",
      "offset": 659,
      "edited": "2026-11-02T01:01:19.897Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-02T01:02:41.926Z",
          "html": "<p>Done:  the Admin space list and \"Rules of the Roles\" are now markdown tables, with the bold part in the left column in <code>&lt;nobr&gt;</code>.</p>\n<p>A markdown table has to have a header row, so each has a thin empty one on top.  The longest left cell, \"Admins, the Board and Team Leads can't be removed by Project Leads\", makes that table's left column wide.  I also fixed \"by a Project Leads\" there.</p>"
        }
